  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 330 AM EST Sunday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry and seasonably cool the remainder of the \nweekend through the middle of the week.\n\nGOES water vapor channels depict zonal flow across the Mid- \nAtlantic between a trough departing to the NE over New England \nand another trough diving to the the upper Midwest. At the \nsurface, a broad area of 1024mb high pressure is centered over \nAR and extends eastward into the southern Mid-Atlantic and \nSoutheast. Mostly clear early this morning with some thin cirrus\nspreading across southern VA and NE NC. Temperatures are \ngenerally in the upper 30s to lower 40s inland, with mid 40s to \nlower 50s toward and along the coast.\n\nSurface high pressure settles S today with a lee-side trough \ndeveloping in advance of an upper trough swinging across the \nGreat Lakes. This will allow the low-level flow to become SW by \nthis afternoon under a sunny sky with high temperatures rising \ninto the mid 60s inland, with highs closer to the coast \ngenerally around 60F or in the lower 60s. By tonight, the trough\npushes toward New England with a dry cold front approaching \nfrom the N. Not as cool tonight with lows generally in the \nlower/mid 40s, with some upper 30s in the Piedmont.\n\nThe dry cold front pushes across the area Monday. Becoming \nbreezy behind the front with a NW wind of 10-15 mph with gusts \nto ~20 mph for most of the area, and 15-20 mph with gusts to \n25-30 mph over the Eastern Shore. High pressure rebuilds across \nthe region Monday night into Tuesday, before sliding offshore \nWednesday. High temperatures Monday range from around 60F at the\ncoast to the mid 60s inland, and then mid 60s to around 70F \nTuesday and Wednesday. Lows Monday night/early Tuesday morning \nrange from the mid to upper 30s inland to the lower/mid 40s at \nthe coast. Patchy frost is possible inland, but it is worth \nnoting that the legacy MOS guidance is a few degrees higher with\novernight lows than the NBM. Not as cool but still seasonal \nTuesday night/early Wednesday morning with lows in the upper 30s\nto mid 40s (mid/upper 40s at the coast). Dry weather continues \nthrough Wednesday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...The weather pattern becomes more amplified \nlater next week with low-end rain chances returning later \nThursday/Thursday night followed by a brief surge of colder air \nwith below average temperatures into next weekend.\n\n08/00z EPS, GEFS, and GEPS all continue to depict an anomalous \n500mb trough developing across the Southeast and Mid-Atlantic by\nThursday- Friday. Low-end rain chances return later \nThursday/Thursday night with the potential for developing low \npressure ahead of the deepening trough. Confidence in details at\nthis forecast range is low, but the EPS, GEFS, and GEPS show a \n40-80% chance of at least 0.1\" of rain through 12z Friday, with \nthe highest chances in the N, and only a 10-20% chance of at \nleast 0.5\" of rain across the N. Looking at percentiles, the \n50th percentile QPF is generally 0.1\" or less S to 0.1-0.2\" N, \nwith a reasonable low end (10th percentile) of a T to a few \nhundredths of an inch, and a reasonable high end (90th \npercentile) of ~0.25\" S to ~0.5\" N. A surge of colder air \nfollows with below average temperatures favored by Friday into \nnext weekend. With this trend, there is a chance this could end \nthe growing season for the remaining areas.",
